3 CITY Degernes
3 STAE Ostfold
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 DEC 1933
2 ADDR
3 CITY Degernes
3 STAE Ostfold
3 CTRY Norway
1 BURI
2 DATE JAN 1934
2 ADDR
3 STAE Ostfold
3 CTRY Norway
1 OCCU slakter, bonde, telefonsentraloperatør etc.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes: (from www.pinaas.no – by Johannes’s daughter Karen Dingtorp)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1899 Johannes was born at Haug, Degernes.  He had an older brother named Martin who took over the farm Haug. He also had several half siblings. Johannes took over Aasen farm around 1889 together with his wife Maren Oline. They had
2 CONC  4-5 cows, a horse, pigs, chickens and turkeys. It was difficult to work the farm’s fields, the building was located on top of the hill (Aasen) and the field ran downhill on all sides. They had a mortgage on the farm and very litt
2 CONC le money while the family grew. Maren Oline gave birth to a son during the Christmas Season 1898. The child only lived for 8 hours. The child’s grandmother Haug baptized the son and named him Kristian. Maren Oline died in January 
2 CONC 1899. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes had a difficult time after his wife Maren’s death. He had 5 young children that all needed attention. He could not afford to hire a housekeeper, so he went looking for a new wife. He got in touch with a 30 year old lady, 
2 CONC Elise Stentorp, and they got married. Elise agreed to the marriage providing that she did not have to raise his children, so Johannes had to get rid of his children somehow. However – Elise was willing to keep one child, and it wa
2 CONC s Karen that got to stay. (The other 4 kids was placed with other families in the community) The estate was split and each child inherited approximately 80 kroner (about $14 in 2013 currency) and the 5 children got “distributed” a
2 CONC s follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. Hans, 15 years old, got mad at the father and ran away to Kristiania
2 CONT *2. Anna, 12 years old, found work as a domestic/farm helper at Lilleheier Farm, Degernes
2 CONT *3. Ole, 9 years old, got to stay with his legal guardian Kristen Titterud at Titterud Farm, Degernes
2 CONT *4. Karen, 6 years old, got to stay with her father and stepmother at Aasen Farm, Degernes.
2 CONT *5. Magna, 3 years old, got to stay with her aunt (Johannes’ sister) Lovise
2 CONT
2 CONT 1901 Johannes soon had to give up farming at Aasen and sold the farm to his half-sister Mathilde and Karl Haug. Johannes and Maren then moved to Paulsrud Farm, Degernes and rented the building Nygård.  This was around 1901. Johann
2 CONC es got work in a mine where he worked for a year or so, but he did not like this kind of work. He then bought a small farm, Dingtorp, located next to the main road in the center of Degernes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes’ daughter Karen’s memories from Dingtorp:  The main building was gray, unpainted, with small windows. The windows had covers that could be closed during bad weather and winter-time, then it became warm and cozy indoors. W
2 CONC e had a large kitchen, large living room, and a tiny little sitting room. In this tiny room we cemented in a huge oven for baking.  The opening to insert wood for burning was in the kitchen. In the kitchen we had a built in bunk b
2 CONC ed. This was common in most homes at the time. Karen slept in the upper bunk with Magna (who stayed with us at Dingtorp for awhile). There was an old garden outside, some old apple trees, berry bushes, and other trees.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karen continues: I remember when my father would haul timber with the horse in the forest winter time; he would get some extra good food in the morning before leaving. He would get his favorite food “stekt flesk” fried pork meat. 
2 CONC Magna and I would lie in the upper bunk bed watching him eat, hoping that he would not eat it all, so that we could have a taste later. “Stekt flesk” was everyone’s favorite food.
2 CONT
2 CONT One day when my father worked in the forest hauling timber, our horse Borka returned home alone. We then understood that something bad had happened. Part of the “winter road” went across a lake, the ice broke, and the load of timb
2 CONC er went through the ice. My father got the horse freed from the load, the horse got spooked and ran back home by itself.  My father made it safely home later.  My father got a hernia from the heavy work in the woods.  Two doctors 
2 CONC came from Rakkestad and did surgery on my father in our kitchen.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father built the”stabbur”, storage building, on Dingtorp Farm, and then the money ran out.  We lived at Dingtorp 4-5 years, Karen recalls. My father worked hard to cover the cost on the farm. The buildings were old and lots of 
2 CONC money was needed to rebuild both the main house and the barn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes decided to sell the Dingtorp Farm, it was sold at a”voluntary auction”, after my father parted out acreage for his own use. My father built a small house, a work shop and a storage building.  Dingtorp Farm, including anim
2 CONC als and farming equipment and machinery, was bought by Inga and Edvard Langeløkke.  My father then gave up farming.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1904-1905. The telephone central in Degernes needed to be moved at that time and it was us that got to house it in our new house. This was around 1904-1905 when I was 12 years old. Our new house reminded me of a doll house, the ki
2 CONC tchen was painted light red and the sitting room light green. This was where the switch board was located before an addition to the house was built and the switch board moved outside our living area.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father started a garden and planted trees around the house. We were one of the first families that got electricity, this was around 1908, and my father got electricity to his work shop as well. The telephone central had to stay
2 CONC  open 24 hours a day. There was about 100 customer to start, but this number increased rapidly. We could never stay away from home.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father built a small house to raise honey bees in; he also made bee hives that he sold nation wide. The bee hives were delivered to Rakkestad Stasjon and distributed all over the country. My father was mentioned as a factory ow
2 CONC ner in the magazine for bee owners. My stepmother and I helped producing the frames for the bee hives.  My father extended his work shop to make garden furniture, benches, etc. and had several interns working for him.  
2 CONT
2 CONT My father also worked as a community butcher, and many farmers called on him as he was known to treat the animals well. He charged 1 krone (about 15 US cents in today’s 2013 currency) to butcher a pig, and he could do up to 8 of t
2 CONC hem per day.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1914 Johannes got rewarded with a medal at “Norges Jubilæumsutstilling” in Kristiania in 1914 for his excellent honey quality. This medal in now in at Egil Berger’s home in Texas, USA. (Johannes is Egil’s great grandfather)
2 CONT
2 CONT My stepmother Elise was strict, difficult and not very nice to me. She was strong, something I felt when she beat me. What was most difficult for me to handle was that she did not speak the truth to my father about us children. Sh
2 CONC e made sure that my father did not send any money to any of his other children.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
